"45d_plasma.mst — the direct framebuffer, proven by the hardest case for a
 command protocol: a full-screen plasma where every pixel changes every frame
 (docs/shared_screen_memory_design.md, SM0). Launch it from the Demos menu, or
 a Workspace:

     Plasma launch

 SPACE cycles the palette, Escape quits.

 WHAT THIS DEMO IS FOR. Every other demo here ships its pixels: MandelZoom and
 Life fill a ByteArray and hand it over with `blit:`, which is one command a
 frame and perfectly good — but it still costs three full-frame copies before
 the GPU sees anything (out of the Smalltalk heap, through the queue, into the
 pane's buffer, up to the texture). This demo sends NO pixels at all. It asks
 the pane for `screenMemory` and writes into it, and because that memory is a
 Metal shared buffer the GPU samples directly, the bytes it stores ARE the
 screen. On Apple Silicon the graphics memory is just memory; nothing here is
 copying it about.

 The measurable claim, and the regression test that holds it: a frame of this
 demo emits exactly ONE command — the `present` that says 'show it' — no
 matter how many pixels changed. Filling 76,800 of them adds nothing to the
 channel.

 TWO RULES FOR WRITING DIRECT PIXELS, both easy to get wrong and both worth
 more than a comment apiece:

 1. THE STRIDE IS NOT THE WIDTH. A row is `screenStride` bytes, because the
    GPU rounds the row up to its own alignment. Address `y * stride + x`. If
    you use `y * width + x` the picture shears diagonally, and it will look
    like your maths is wrong when it is your addressing.

 2. REFETCH `screenMemory` EVERY FRAME. Presenting rotates which of three
    buffers you are writing — that rotation is exactly what stops you drawing
    into the buffer the GPU is currently reading — so an Alien kept from last
    frame points at the wrong one.

 The colour is a palette index, as everywhere else in this engine, so the
 plasma's motion comes free: the field itself is static per frame and SPACE
 just rolls the 256-entry palette underneath it."
"I am a full-screen plasma drawn straight into GPU memory — the demo that shows
 a pixel write does not need to be a message. I ask my pane for screenMemory
 and store palette indices into it; nothing copies them. Launch me from the
 Demos menu, or a Workspace: Plasma launch. SPACE cycles my palette, Escape
 quits. I address rows by screenStride, never by width, and I refetch
 screenMemory every frame because presenting rotates the buffer. In a headless
 VM screenMemory answers nil and I do nothing, harmlessly."
Object subclass: Plasma [
    | pane w h phase palShift prevSpace |
    <classVars: Current>

    Plasma class >> launch [ ^self new start ]
    Plasma class >> current [ ^Current ]

    start [
        Current := self.
        w := 320.
        h := 240.
        pane := GamePane new.
        pane openDirect: w height: h.
        phase := 0.
        palShift := 0.
        prevSpace := false.
        self buildPalette.
        pane onStep: [ :frame | self stepWith: frame ]; run.
        ^self
    ]

    buildPalette [
        "A smooth 256-entry cycle through the spectrum. Because the pixels are
         palette INDICES, rolling this palette animates the whole screen
         without touching a single pixel — the oldest trick in demo graphics,
         and it still costs nothing."
        | k r g b |
        k := 0.
        [ k < 256 ] whileTrue: [
            r := (self wave: k) .
            g := (self wave: k + 85).
            b := (self wave: k + 170).
            pane paletteAt: k r: r g: g b: b.
            k := k + 1 ].
        ^self
    ]

    wave: k [
        "A cheap triangle wave 0..255 over a period of 256 — a sine would be
         prettier and this is a palette, not a physics engine."
        | v |
        v := k \\ 256.
        v > 127 ifTrue: [ v := 255 - v ].
        ^v * 2
    ]

    stepWith: frame [
        "One frame: read SPACE, compute the field into screen memory, present.
         `present` is the ONLY command this method emits."
        | fb stride |
        self readSpaceFrom: frame.
        fb := pane screenMemory.
        "Headless (or the web GUI, which has no shared buffer): nothing to
         write into, so do nothing rather than fail."
        fb isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        stride := pane screenStride.
        self paintInto: fb stride: stride.
        phase := phase + 2.
        self drawHud.
        pane present.
        ^self
    ]

    drawHud [
        "A HUD on the TEXT PLANE, rewritten from scratch EVERY frame — which
         is the point. Through the old text overlay this was a command per
         string per frame, and the defence against that cost was remembering
         to redraw only when something changed. Here it is four stores per
         character into shared memory, so redrawing everything always is
         simply cheaper than deciding whether to."
        pane textPut: 'DIRECT GPU MEMORY' at: 1 row: 1 color: GamePane white.
        pane textPut: 'FRAME ', phase printString at: 1 row: 2 color: GamePane yellow.
        pane textPut: 'SPACE CYCLES PALETTE   ESC QUITS'
             at: 1 row: 28 color: GamePane lightGrey.
        ^self
    ]

    paintInto: fb stride: stride [
        "The plasma proper: a sum of two travelling waves, one horizontal and
         one diagonal, indexed into the palette. Written straight into GPU
         memory one byte at a time — 76,800 stores, and not one of them is a
         message."
        | y x rowBase v |
        y := 0.
        [ y < h ] whileTrue: [
            rowBase := y * stride.
            x := 0.
            [ x < w ] whileTrue: [
                v := (self wave: x + phase)
                        + (self wave: y + phase)
                        + (self wave: x + y + (phase * 2)).
                fb byteAt: rowBase + x + 1 put: ((v // 3 + palShift) \\ 256).
                x := x + 1 ].
            y := y + 1 ].
        ^self
    ]

    readSpaceFrom: frame [
        "SPACE rolls the palette — edge-triggered, or holding it would spin the
         colours at 60 Hz and look like a fault."
        | down |
        down := frame keyHeld: GamePane keyA.
        (down and: [ prevSpace not ]) ifTrue: [ palShift := (palShift + 32) \\ 256 ].
        prevSpace := down.
        ^self
    ]
]
